    unit_outage_window_hour_grain: { cell: "K", ev: "KEEPER 2026-09-25-r-5-hour-grain (R-ERCOT-5, promoted 2026-09-26 on owner instruction \"If so plz promote\"): 7 shards at d20ca118 on the r-4-day-guard recipe + this field only; train tier CALIBRATED (C3a 2023 -4.6 -> -7.5%, 2024 +0.2 -> -5.6%, 2025 -5.4 -> -6.9%; C3b 0.122/0.116/0.097 -> 0.133/0.120/0.108); validation 2019 C3a +99.4 -> +59.8%, C3b 1.837 -> 1.334; 2020 +19.0 -> +11.7%; 2021 +5.8 -> +2.6%. RESULT docs/handoffs/RESULT-r-ercot-5-hour-grain-2026-09-26.md. || R-ERCOT-5 (2026-09-25) \u2014 O: ARMED FOR TEST on ERCOT through an ERCOT branch of the SAME field (rule 19): ERCOT arms neither per-unit flag, so the nyiso-229 predicate never reached it; for ERCOT the field now selects the '-hourgrain' companions of ERCOT's OWN three window families (campd-unit-outages[-short|-shortgas]-hourgrain.csv, derived from ERCOT CAMPD with --hour-grain; base-column projection == incumbent line-for-line; no number crosses from NYISO/CAISO, rule 25). Zero-LP phase 0 on ERCOT's own CAMPD (NYISO's step 1): 99% of ERCOT windows are contradicted by CEMS for the very unit they declare out, on the first/last day (interior 1-2.5% of unit-hours): 2019 std 17,577 unit-h / 2.89 TWh, short-gas 9,525 / 1.39 TWh, short-coal 725 / 0.26 TWh generated INSIDE their own windows; the same every year 2019-2025. Step 3 (tight hours exist): 2019 P1 has 72 h > $1k + 6,667 MWh slack; the arm restores ~0.7 GW mean in those hours (coal +148, CC +277, ST +269 MW). Also found: the unit-outage deriver lost every coal window to the COAL-SUB relabel (repaired through artifact_class; the incumbent ERCOT std/layup/short/shortgas extracts reproduce line-for-line 2019+). Records: docs/handoffs/FINDING-r-ercot-5-2019-scarcity-2026-09-25.md, docs/handoffs/PRECOMMIT-r-ercot-5-window-hour-grain-2026-09-25.md, scripts/probes/_r_ercot5_window_edge_census.py, scripts/probes/_r_ercot5_availability_census.py, docs/handoffs/r-ercot/r_ercot5_*." },
