    gas_hub_basis_overlay: { cell: "U", ev: "UNTESTED and UNARMABLE FOR LACK OF DATA — the row's gas_hub_basis_daily leg is the named successor to ercot_ep_gas_basis_monthly (rule 19 [R-ONE-MECH]) and is blocked on intake, not on modelling. ercot-265 (2026-09-09, ZERO LP, nothing armed) SURVEYED THE EXTERNAL SIDE AND IT IS EMPTY, structurally: the EIA NGWU daily spot table that four ISOs already scrape carries EXACTLY FOUR ROWS (Henry Hub, New York, Chicago, Cal. Comp. Avg.) and NO Texas hub in any issue — verified directly, re-confirming the ercot-160/163 free-EIA fence rather than re-litigating it; CME/NYMEX Waha and HSC are delisted on zero open interest (ercot-224); ICE/NGI/Platts/Argus are paid and refused by the owner ruling 2026-08-04 (NO PAID DAILY GAS DATA). The two ercot-224 reopen conditions — a new FREE daily Waha/HSC source, or the owner reversing that ruling — are both still false, so NO new screen is owed (28a). WHAT ercot-265 ADDS is a SUFFICIENCY measurement the lane did not have: inverting the keeper's own committed Feb-2021 hourlies for the delivered gas path that would match actual (ORDC and dispatch held fixed) gives a burn-weighted February gas price of $56.79/MMBtu at the repo's own marginal HR 7.36, against the INDEPENDENTLY measured EIA N3045TX3 Feb-2021 print of $59.73 — a 4.9% gap, with the measured print inside the band across HR 6.0-10.0 and landing at -0.9% at HR 7.0. The agreement is informative rather than circular because the monthly mean is a number the inversion never saw. The implied storm-day path ($165-240/MMBtu on Feb 13-17) sits INSIDE the primary-source Texas hub record for those days ($64 Waha to $400 HSC, EIA NGWU 2021-02-18 quoting NGI). CONFIRMED QUANTITATIVELY, from the keeper's own array: Henry Hub CANNOT substitute — gas_daily_shape is already armed here (cell K) and gives February a $10.20 mean peaking at $47.90 on Feb 17, but HH's Feb-2021 dynamic range is 8.3x against the Texas hubs' ~133x, so any mean-preserving reshape that reaches the storm days forces ~$29/MMBtu onto the 23 calm days (ercot-258's refutation, now measured). DECLARED LIMITS: first-order/partial-equilibrium; one system-wide HR; and 35.4% of the required increment falls in hours whose ORDC adder already exceeds $500, where fuel cost is partly clipped, so the true margin is thinner than 4.9%. THE INVERTED PATH IS A DIAGNOSTIC YARDSTICK AND MUST NEVER BE USED AS AN INPUT (rule 13 [R-MEASURED]: it is derived from the residual it would close). Cell stays U: nothing was solved, armed or registered. Record: docs/RESULT-ercot265-daily-gas-survey-sufficiency-2026-09-09.md." },
    mustrun_chp_btm_holdout: { cell: "U", ev: "miso-253 (2026-09-10): row minted with the field (rule 28 duty (c)). UNTESTED HERE. The injection is ISO-GENERIC -- _must_run_profiles runs for every ISO -- so this is a REAL cell, not n/a, and the measured footprint is nonzero: 2023 0.0001 / 2024 0.000 / 2025 0.000 TWh of chp=Y biomass (0.02 / 0.00 / 0.00 %) -- ERCOT's biomass fleet is essentially all non-CHP, so the biomass leg is near-inert here; the OTHER leg is unmeasured. Rule 25 [R-ISO-SCOPE]: MISO's charter and its EIA-930 OTH corroboration transfer to NOBODY; this ISO's cell opens only on its own market's evidence, and the OTH comparator in particular does NOT generalise -- measured across all seven ISOs the model-vs-OTH ratio ranges from 0.55 (PJM 2025, model BELOW telemetry) to NEGATIVE OTH in CAISO (a known 930 artifact), so an ISO adopting this must establish its own grid-side authority first. Default off, byte-identical off; this ISO's keeper is unchanged and unaffected." },
